@@ -51,7 +51,8 @@ So customizations go into `.deb` packages under `packages/`, not into files drop
   - `flickos-menu`: start menu (`usr/bin/flickos-menu`, single Python 3 file, GTK 3 gtk-layer-shell, CSS `usr/share/flickos/menu/style.css` in GTK theme colors only). Its kind follows the layout (`MenuStyle=` in `layout.ini`, second line of `menu/anchor`): `classic` Windows 7 (Redmond), `categories` GNOME 2/Whisker (Traditional), `grid` full-screen Launchpad (Cupertino), unless the user picked one for all layouts (`flickos-layout menu-style list|current|set layout|classic|categories|grid`, `MenuStyle=` in `layouts.conf`, default `layout`; *Start menu style* on the Settings page, active only while the apps button is the menu); one `View` subclass each (`VIEWS`, same names as `MENU_STYLES` in both packages), only the current one built; `set` sends a running daemon SIGWINCH so it builds the new layout's view while hidden. **On by default** since flickos-layouts 1.23 (`DEFAULT_LAUNCHER`, `Launcher=menu`; ADR-010, an exception recorded in HIG INT-9; fuzzel is `FALLBACK_LAUNCHER`, the only choice without flickos-menu); flickos-layouts owns the choice (`flickos-layout launcher list|current|set fuzzel|menu`, `Launcher=` in `layouts.conf`, *Apps button* on flickos-control's layout page, shown while flickos-menu is installed). While on for a layout with `Menu=` (corner), flickos-layout writes `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flickos/menu/anchor`, adds `Super_L`/`Super_R` `onRelease` keybinds (`MENU_SUPER`) to the rc.xml overlay, uses `MENU_CLICK_ACTIONS` for desktop clicks, points the generated `custom/launcher` at it (`MENU_TOGGLE`), and runs `flickos-menu daemon` with the panel under `supervise` (`sync_menu` starts/stops it on `set`); while off none of that exists and nothing runs. The daemon keeps the window built and answers `pkill` signals (SIGUSR1 toggle, SIGUSR2 Super tap, SIGWINCH hide; GLib sources at default priority, a higher one deadlocks mapping the layer surface); without the anchor file `toggle` runs fuzzel and starts no daemon. A Super tap counts only if Super was alone and let go within `TAP_MS` (below flickos-shortcuts' `HOLD_MS`), timed through GDK's keymap state. Pins: first `flickos/menu.conf` in `XDG_CONFIG_DIRS` (flickos-installer's live one has Install FlickOS), then `~/.config/flickos/menu`. Design and measurements: `docs/design/flickos-menu.md`. Unit tests in `tests/`. A Depends of flickos-desktop and in `REQUIRED`.
   - `flickos-welcome`: the window a new account sees at login on an installed system, until it ticks *Do not show on startup* (`flickos-welcome startup current|set on|off|default`) (`usr/bin/flickos-welcome`, single Python 3 file, GTK 3 via python3-gi, no CSS; *Welcome* in the apps menu via `flickos-welcome.desktop`). Cards (`CARDS`, hidden when their program is missing) run the program that owns the job: `flickos-layout pick`, `flickos-control`, `nm-connection-editor`, `flickos-shortcuts show`, `gpk-application`, and `xdg-open` for the addresses in the conffile `/etc/xdg/flickos/welcome.conf` (`[Links]`; a card whose address is empty is left out, and `DEFAULT_LINKS` keeps `donate` empty so a payment address is never baked into the program. **`Donate=` must stay the same address as the installer branding's `donateUrl`**; a unit test compares them). `Enabled=no` turns it off for the machine. It owns no settings. `first-run` (flickos-settings' autostart) opens it while the marker `~/.config/flickos/welcome` exists (from `/etc/skel`, lintian override; the check box deletes/writes it atomically, `boot=live` and `Enabled=no` delete it), and it also deletes flickos-layouts' `choose-layout` so the chooser it replaces can't open too; never with `boot=live`. autostart runs `flickos-welcome first-run` when installed and `flickos-layout first-run` only otherwise, so exactly one window opens. Unit tests in `tests/`. A Depends of flickos-desktop and in `REQUIRED`.
   - `flickos-phone`: pages, files and text between an Android phone with the KDE Connect app and the computer (`usr/bin/flickos-phone`, single Python 3 file, stdlib only, no GTK except `send --choose`'s file chooser; design and phase 0 results with a real phone: `docs/design/flickos-phone.md`). Speaks only KDE Connect protocol 8's pairing, `kdeconnect.share.request`, `kdeconnect.clipboard`, `kdeconnect.ping`, plus `kdeconnect.battery` in (the button's tooltip) and `kdeconnect.findmyphone.request` out (`ring`) (`INCOMING`, `OUTGOING`). **Off by default** (`flickos-phone set enabled yes|no|default`, `[Phone]` in the conffile `/etc/xdg/flickos/phone.conf` then `~/.config/flickos/phone`; `record()` for History), never with `boot=live`; autostart runs `flickos-phone daemon`, which exits at once while off (`daemon.lock`, control socket `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flickos/phone/control`, JSON lines). In KDE Connect the TCP connector is the TLS *server*, so the daemon never connects to a phone's main port (it broadcasts to UDP 1716 only while no phone is connected, since every broadcast makes a phone reconnect, and answers a phone's broadcast by UDP): it is always the TLS client with `CERT_NONE` and checks certificates itself (`cert_fields` parses the DER for CN and SPKI, `verification_key`); paired certificates pinned in `$XDG_DATA_HOME/flickos/phone/devices/`. Files sent to the phone: the daemon is TLS server with `CERT_REQUIRED` and only the pinned certificate, retried once. Listens only while the active NetworkManager connection is in `Networks` (pairing adds it; `nmcli monitor`) or while `flickos-phone pair` runs; pair requests are refused otherwise. `state` (one JSON object for *Settings → Phone*), `pair --json` (its *Add Phone…*), `set networks 'UUID …'|default`. Panel button `custom/phone` (defined in flickos-layouts' `common/modules.jsonc`; `use_phone()` puts it before `custom/tiler` in the generated panel only while `phone_on()`, i.e. `flickos-phone get enabled` is yes and not live; `set enabled` runs `flickos-layout reload-panel`): `status --panel` JSON (dimmed class `waiting`), the daemon sends `SIGRTMIN+PANEL_SIGNAL` (9; tiler's is 8) on phone connect/disconnect, click = `flickos-phone menu` (fuzzel: *Send Files to Phone…* = `send --choose`, GTK's file chooser imported only there; *Send Text to Phone…* = `send --type-text`, one line in fuzzel `--prompt-only`; *Send Clipboard to Phone*, where `send --clipboard` sends a web address as a page; *Send Screenshot to Phone* = `send --screenshot`, grim into the runtime folder, deleted after, only while grim is installed; *Ring My Phone* = `ring`; the newest `MENU_RECEIVED` items; *Phone Settings* = `flickos-control --page phone`), tooltip with the phone's battery (`battery_text`). *Send to Phone* in PCManFM: `usr/share/file-manager/actions/flickos-phone-send.desktop` (`send --notify %F`, the result as a notification); libfm ignores `ShowIfTrue`/`ShowIfRunning` and honours only `TryExec` (searched on `PATH`), so the daemon keeps the link `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flickos/bin/flickos-phone-on` (`ON_LINK`) while it runs and flickos-session puts that folder last on `PATH`; actions need `libfm-modules` (a Depends, dropped from the image as libfm's Recommends). Received items: notification whose click (mako shows no buttons) opens a page/openable file or copies text, `received.jsonl` (last `RECEIVED_ITEMS`, `RECEIVED_DAYS`), files into Downloads (cleaned name, never overwritten, never executable). ufw drops incoming by default: phones need TCP 1716 and 1739–1764. Unit tests with a fake phone on 127.0.0.1 in `tests/`. A Depends of flickos-desktop and in `REQUIRED`.
-  - `flickos-control`: the Settings window (`usr/bin/flickos-control`, single Python 3 file, GTK 3 via python3-gi, no CSS; *Settings → All Settings*). Owns the mouse, touchpad and keyboard settings (`SETTINGS`; `flickos-control get|set KEY VALUE|prepare`; `~/.config/flickos/input`, `keyboard`, system defaults in the conffiles `/etc/xdg/flickos/input.conf`, `keyboard.conf`): `set` writes `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flickos/control/labwc.xml` (`<libinput>` categories touchpad/non-touch, `<keyboard>` options) and `control/environment` (all four `XKB_DEFAULT_*`), then runs `flickos-layout reconfigure`; flickos-session runs `flickos-control prepare` before `flickos-layout prepare`. labwc never resets an option that disappears, so a set value stays written and `set KEY default` stores the default. Only keys labwc 0.8.3 parses (tests hold the list). The system layout (`/etc/default/keyboard`; Debian's localed is read-only) goes through `pkexec /usr/libexec/flickos/control-helper keyboard …` (polkit `org.flickos.control.policy`, one action per verb via `exec.argv1`). Locking is `flickos-control lock` (swaylock `SWAYLOCK` plus, with `lock.background` `wallpaper`, the images the user's swaybg shows, per output); swayidle runs it, and flickos-settings' `/usr/libexec/flickos/lock` (Super+L, both menus, flickos-menu; plain swaylock without flickos-control); `idle.lock-before-sleep`. Also owns the idle times (`idle.*`, `~/.config/flickos/idle`, `/etc/xdg/flickos/idle.conf`): autostart runs `flickos-control idle` (execs swayidle with `FLICKOS_IDLE=1`, which also locks on logind's `lock`; never with `boot=live`), and a change restarts only that swayidle. *Power & Idle* also has the power button and lid (logind's `Handle*` read with `busctl`; the helper's `power SETTING ACTION` writes `/etc/systemd/logind.conf.d/60-flickos.conf` and reloads logind; lid rows only with `SW_LID`). Language and formats (`locale.language`, `locale.formats`, `~/.config/flickos/locale`, generated locales only) become `LANG`, `LANGUAGE=`, `LC_MESSAGES` and `FORMAT_VARS` lines in `control/environment`; *Language & Region* sets the system's with `localectl set-locale` (trixie's localed can) and generates a missing locale through the helper's `locale-add` (`/etc/locale.gen`, `locale-gen --keep-existing`). Night light (`night-light.enabled`, default on, `night-light.temperature` = wlsunset `-t`; on *Displays*): autostart runs `flickos-control night-light` (execs flickos-settings' `night-light` script, which passes options to wlsunset, with `FLICKOS_NIGHT_LIGHT=1`; nothing while off), a change restarts only FlickOS's wlsunset. Do Not Disturb (`notifications.do-not-disturb`, *Notifications* page) is mako's `do-not-disturb` mode from flickos-settings' mako config (critical still shown); `makoctl` runs only while the user's mako owns `org.freedesktop.Notifications` (mako's D-Bus activation would start a plain mako), and autostart's `flickos-control notifications` re-adds the mode after login when on. Startup applications (*Startup Applications*, `flickos-control startup list|enable|disable|add|remove|run`): XDG autostart by the spec (user folder first, `Hidden`, `OnlyShowIn`, `TryExec`, `Exec` field codes, `X-GNOME-Autostart-enabled=false` = off, a system entry changed through a user copy); autostart runs `startup run` last (waits ≤10 s for the tray); flickos-settings' `/etc/xdg/flickos/autostart` hides the entries of what its autostart starts itself and keeps the print applet off, so nothing new starts by default. Automatic updates (*Updates*, installed only; `flickos-control updates` prints them) are read from `apt-config dump`; the helper's `updates auto|scope|reboot VALUE` writes `/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/60flickos-updates` (`#clear`s the origins; scopes `UPDATE_SCOPES` in both files). flickos-settings Breaks flickos-control (<< 1.10). *Displays* keeps screen arrangements: `flickos-control displays list|save|forget|start` writes one kanshi profile per set of connected screens (`wlr-randr --json`) into `~/.config/flickos/displays`; autostart's `displays start` execs kanshi with `FLICKOS_DISPLAYS=1` only while one is kept and no other kanshi runs, and `save`/`forget` SIGHUP, start or stop it (Depends kanshi, wlr-randr). *Default Applications* runs `flickos-control default-app set` (`xdg-mime default`, or `~/.config/xdg-terminals.list` for the terminal); Super+Enter/the menu run `xdg-terminal-exec` (FlickOS default foot in flickos-settings' `/etc/xdg/flickos/xdg-terminals.list`), Super+B/Super+E/the menu run flickos-settings' `/usr/libexec/flickos/open-default browser|files`. *Date & Time* runs `timedatectl` (flickos-desktop depends on `systemd-timesyncd`; systemd only recommends it). *Login* (installed systems with flickos-greeter's `autologin` group only) runs the helper's `autologin-on`/`autologin-off`, for the calling user only (`PKEXEC_UID`). *Users* (installed systems only) runs its `user-add NAME FULLNAME yes|no`, `user-remove NAME keep|delete`, `user-admin NAME yes|no`, `user-password NAME` (passwords on stdin; adduser/chpasswd/userdel/gpasswd; administrators = group `sudo`; new accounts get the installer's `defaultGroups`, `DEFAULT_GROUPS`; never the caller's account, a logged-in one or the last administrator). *Firewall* (while `/etc/ufw/ufw.conf` exists; ufw is only a Recommends of flickos-desktop) reads its `ENABLED=` (world-readable; `ufw status` needs root) and runs the helper's `firewall-on`/`firewall-off` (`ufw --force enable`/`ufw disable`); rules stay `sudo ufw`. *Phone* (Devices group; while flickos-phone is installed, not with `boot=live`) owns no settings: it reads one `flickos-phone state` (again every `PHONE_REFRESH_SECONDS` while mapped, the timer stops when it isn't), runs `flickos-phone set|forget|network`, and *Add Phone…* talks to `flickos-phone pair --json` (events on stdout, yes/no on stdin; Cancel before a request kills it, which closes the daemon's pair window). Its firewall row is the system setting `firewall.phones`: the helper's `firewall-phones on|off` writes the ufw app profile `PHONES_PROFILE` (TCP 1716, 1739:1764) and allows it from `PRIVATE_NETWORKS` only, deleting both for off; the profile (first line `PHONES_MARK`, same in both files, a test checks) is the readable state. flickos-phone's `enabled`, `name`, `networks` are in `PHONE_HISTORY_KEYS` (undo = `flickos-phone set KEY OLD`), `device.ID` is shown only. *Memory* (`docs/design/flickos-memory.md`; so far its *This computer* section) shows memory and in use from `/proc/meminfo` and the system setting `firefox.memory`: the helper's `firefox-memory on|off` writes/deletes `/etc/firefox-esr/flickos-memory.js` (`FIREFOX_MEMORY_PREFS`, `pref()` defaults that a user's own values beat; Debian's firefox-esr reads `/etc/firefox-esr/*.js` through its `browser/defaults/syspref` link, and the row is hidden without it), applied at Firefox's next start (`docs/design/flickos-firefox.md`); *Recommended for this computer* when `old_computer()` finds ≤ `OLD_MEMORY_KB` or no accelerated render node (`SOFTWARE_DRIVERS`, virtio without virgl). *History* (`flickos-control history [--days N] [--json]|undo ID|clear`; `docs/design/flickos-history.md`): every owner (this one's `save_values`, flickos-layout's `apply_choice`, flick-tiler's `save_choice`) appends a change that shows to `$XDG_STATE_HOME/flickos/history.jsonl` (0600, `HISTORY_BYTES` then the older half dropped) with its own `record()` in one shared format (`v`, `id` = time_ns, `time`, `owner`, `key`, `old`, `new`; `default` = the user had no value); a failure is only a warning. `shape_history` merges one setting's entries within `MERGE_SECONDS`, drops no-ops, keeps `HISTORY_DAYS`; the page shows `HISTORY_ROWS`. Undo (`undo_commands`) runs the owner's command with the old value (every owner's `set` takes `default`), never a stored command, only for a setting's newest change and while `current_value` still matches. `history.enabled` (on by default under HIG INT-9's exception for records; conffile `history.conf`, also read by the other owners) turns it off and deletes the file (`history clear` also writes `history.cleared`, before which apt's changes are left out). System settings go through `flickos-control system KEY [VALUE]` (`SYSTEM_LABELS`: power button/lid, firewall, `firefox.memory`, autologin, updates, timezone, ntp, `keyboard.system`), which the pages run and which records after success; default apps through `default-app list|set CATEGORY APP`; `startup …` records `startup.ID`; the Users and Language pages record `user.NAME`/`language.LOCALE` (display only, except administrator on/off). Software changes: the helper reads apt's log (`packages-list [DAYS]`, `packages-plan ID`: read-only verbs, `READ_ONLY`, no root, no polkit action) and leaves out the installation (up to the transaction that removes `flickos-installer`/`calamares`, plus `INSTALL_SECONDS`; in the live session everything before boot); `pkexec control-helper packages-undo ID` (ID = `Start-Date` as `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS`) re-reads it as root, simulates with `apt-get -s` (asked-for packages plus the ones they brought in, named, never `--autoremove`; falls back to the asked-for ones), refuses extra removals, anything in flickos-desktop's Depends and `LIVE_PACKAGES`, and runs apt-get with `-o FlickOS::Undo=ID`. The page shows the plan before the password. `history restore WHEN [--dry-run]` (WHEN: a change id = just before it, a date = its end, or a date and time; *Put Back as It Was…* on the page, per day) gives every setting changed since WHEN its value then (`restore_plan`: `restorable()` = no password: own settings, layout, tiler, default and startup apps; not system settings, software, users), runs `restore_commands` (flickos-layout last, in one `set … --style …`), then records `restore` (old = when it started, new = WHEN); `shape_history` shows the changes between those as that one row, whose Undo is `history restore OLD`. Moments compare by `id` (ns). Add a `HISTORY_LABELS` entry (and `can_undo`/`undo_commands` for a new owner, `SYSTEM_LABELS`/`check_system`/`system_value`/`system_command` for a new system setting) when adding a setting. Pages whose class has `available()` false are left out. *About* (`system_info()`; `flickos-control about` prints it plus FlickOS's package versions, for bug reports) copies with `wl-copy`. The sidebar groups the pages under headings (`Page.group`; `PAGES`/`PAGE_CLASSES` in sidebar order, a group's pages together: Desktop layout, tiling, notifications; Devices displays, input, keyboard, shortcuts, idle; Apps apps, startup; System region, datetime, memory, updates; Accounts and security users, login, firewall; then history, more, about with no heading) in a `GtkListBox` (`page_list()`; `GtkStackSidebar` has no headings). Other pages own no settings: they run the owning command (`flickos-layout`, `flick-tiler`, `flickos-shortcuts`); constructors only build widgets, and `shown()` reads through `Ui.background()` (a thread, newest answer per key wins, spinner until then) so the window opens at once; the layout page reads everything with one `flickos-layout state` (JSON, flickos-layouts >= 1.19); changes run with `run_async`; a missing capability goes into the owner (hence `flick-tiler floating list|add|remove`, `gap N`, and `ratio`/`gap` in its status, flick-tiler >= 1.3). *More Settings* tiles (`TILES`, same apps as the Settings menu) are hidden when the app is missing. Search box above the pages: `Ui.row()`/`Ui.heading()` index what pages build while the window opens (`ui.indexing`), plus pages, tiles and `SEARCH_KEYWORDS` (one entry per page and tile, a test checks); `find` folds case and accents; a result scrolls to the row and focuses its control, or starts the tile's app. Add keywords when adding a page or tile. One window per session (`control.lock`). Design, ADRs and the phase plan (input, keyboard layout, idle, login next): `docs/design/flickos-control.md`. Unit tests in `tests/`. A Depends of flickos-desktop and in `REQUIRED`.
